    "summary": "Historic Feb 18 '71 Capitol show – first‑time debuts & monster Dark Star jam",
    "review": "The February 18, 1971 night at the Capitol Theater is one of those landmark shows that still feels fresh every time you spin it. The band rolled out a slew of premieres – Bertha, Greatest Story Ever Told and Playin' In The Band all made their public debut, and the setlist races from a tight first set into a second‑set marathon anchored by the legendary Dark Star → Wharf Rat → Dark Star “beautiful jam.” Jerry’s soaring tone, wah‑wah licks on Sugar Magnolia and an emotional, searing solo through the Star keep the whole thing on fire, while Phil’s bass locks in with Bob for some serious “Phil bombs” that have fans moving to tears. Mickey Hart’s gũiro adds a tribal edge, and Ned Lagin’s clavichord/e‑piano textures give the jams an otherworldly hue.\n\nWhat makes this show especially listenable is the quality of the source material – the Cantor‑Crouch‑Diebert soundboard capture (the community’s top pick) delivers crisp 1971 board tone with superb balance, letting every nuance from Pigpen’s organ backdrop to the dual‑drum interplay shine. Whether you’re hunting the first‑time moments or the epic Dark Star excursion, this Capitol performance is a high‑octane snapshot of the Dead at a creative peak.",
